This folder is being used to run tests for a new approach to establishing the semantics of behaviors. The objective is to truly automate debugging by creating a semantically executable model of behavior that defines the authoritative standard of correctness.

Behavior consists of:
- Preconditions (participants, invarants)
- Transformation
- Postconditions (participants, invariants)

The preconditions are loaded from the log and the invariants are enforced.
The transformation is applied to the state and the authoritative post conditions are derived.
The postconditiosn are evaluated for correctness and invariants are enforced.

So in this model, there are three different ways to be semantically invalid:
- Preconditions invalid (invariant violated)
- Transformation invalid (implementation fails to implement the semantic definition)
- Postcondition invalid (invariant violated)

An interesting note: 
If the preconditions are valid and the transformation is valid according to the semantics but the post condition is semantically invalid, then this points to an internally inconsistent design. This is another kind of feedback that the engine can provide, it lets to debug the design itself by identifying internal inconsistencies.

### Semantic Primitives

So now I can create a set of primitives, these are computable state transformations.

```text
set <value> <target>
insert <value> <target> <position>
remove <value> <target>
add <value> <target>
divide <left> <right> <target>
sum <source> <target>
length <source> <target>
```

### Derived Primitives
Then I can use these primitives to create, larger ones, like Average:
```text
average scores result
```
becomes 
```text
sum scores total
length scores count
divide total count result
```

Now the behaviors are computable, the engine can realize the state transformatins descrived by the behavior and verify that the implementation is faithful to the semantic model of the behavior.

### Composite Behavior

```text
Start Scheduler

Transformation:
    set scheduler status "running"
    set scheduler available true
```

This is a composite behavior that is made up of semantic primitives. It describes an unambiguous state transformation that is computable by the engine.


### Library Manager Planning with Primitives

This plans the primitives needed to implement library manager design with transformations built from primitives. This will be used to establish the automated debugging functionality along with verification of implementation using the executable semantic model.

AcceptBook
- This is very interesting because the environment will submit a value here. The behavior itself add a book to the world state but there is no notion of the correct book. Except ofcourse, the invariants placed on the post behavior state (book name length etc.)
So the engine will also receive a value here, it will accept it from the environment.

PlaceBookInBasket
- insert basket book [] 0

GetBookFromBasket
- getFromPos basket 0 book

GetFirstLetterOfBookName
- get book ["name"] book_name
- getFromPos book_name 0 firstLetter

CreateSlotOnShelf
- set book_shelf empty_slot [<first_letter>]

PlaceBookOnShelf
- insert book book_shelf [<first_letter>] 0

GenerateAuditReport
- create audit <empty_audit>
- set audit basket ["basket"]
- set audit book_shelf ["book_shelf"]